---
import CasePage from '../../components/case/CasePage.astro';
import Section from '../../components/case/Section.astro';
import Plate from '../../components/case/Plate.astro';
import Limits from '../../components/case/Limits.astro';
import FareboxRecovery from '../../components/case/figures/FareboxRecovery.astro';

const figures = [
  ['60.6%', 'Farebox recovery, FY2024-25'],
  ['₹670.48 cr', 'Net loss, FY2024-25'],
  ['₹2,726.78 cr', 'Accumulated losses'],
  ['₹815.96 cr', 'Rise in current liabilities in one year'],
  ['₹51,286 cr', 'Five guarantee schemes, 2026-27'],
  ['12%', 'Share of expenditure excluding debt repayment'],
  ['₹1,081–1,141 cr', 'Estimated Shakti share of traffic revenue'],
  ['27–28%', 'Cash farebox recovery after that adjustment'],
];
---

<CasePage
  num="03"
  domain="Public finance · transport"
  title="Karnataka transit"
  subtitle="What free bus travel costs, and who pays for it."
  abstract="Karnataka's five guarantee schemes cost ₹51,286 crore in 2026-27, 12% of expenditure excluding debt repayment. BMTC recovered 60.6% of its operating cost in FY2024-25 — before the Shakti reimbursement is counted. This case file follows the gap between the two numbers."
  description="An evidence-based study of Karnataka's guarantee schemes, BMTC farebox recovery, and the affordability of public transport."
  year="2026"
  status="Independent research package"
  stack="Python · pandas · Plotly · 31 sources"
  spec={[
    { label: 'Problem', value: 'A scheme that pays for bus travel is also a transport subsidy, and the accounts do not say so.' },
    { label: 'Constraint', value: 'BMTC does not disclose a Shakti line; the November 2026 fare chart was unpublished at the cut-off.' },
    { label: 'Approach', value: '31 sources, seven datasets, verification labels, a bounded estimate and a scenario model.' },
    { label: 'Result', value: 'Shakti is an estimated ₹1,081–1,141 crore of ₹2,039.09 crore traffic revenue; cash recovery is 27–28%.' },
  ]}
  links={[
    { label: 'Code', href: 'https://github.com/AmarthyaT/karnataka-transport-research' },
    { label: 'Report', href: 'https://github.com/AmarthyaT/karnataka-transport-research/blob/main/report/Karnataka_Transport_Research_Report.md' },
    { label: 'Sources', href: 'https://github.com/AmarthyaT/karnataka-transport-research/blob/main/evidence/SOURCES.md' },
  ]}
  contents={[
    { id: 'question', label: 'The question' },
    { id: 'evidence', label: 'The evidence' },
    { id: 'result', label: 'The result' },
    { id: 'fares', label: 'The fare revisions' },
    { id: 'limits', label: 'Known limits' },
  ]}
  sources={[
    { label: 'Audited accounts', note: 'BMTC FY2023-24 and FY2024-25.' },
    { label: 'Source register', note: 'S01–S31, with access dates and labels.' },
    { label: 'Datasets', note: 'Seven CSVs, reproducible generator.' },
  ]}
  next={{ num: '04', title: 'Sweety', href: '/work/sweety' }}
>
  <Section id="question" num="01" label="The question" title="The subsidy is in the wrong column.">
    <p>
      Shakti makes bus travel free for women in Karnataka. It is budgeted as a guarantee scheme,
      reimbursed to the transport corporations, and reported by BMTC as subsidy income rather than as
      passenger revenue. That classification is defensible. It also makes a farebox look healthier
      than the cash position.
    </p>
    <p>
      The question was not whether the scheme is good. It was: what does the published account
      actually show, and what would change if the reimbursement were paid in full?
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="evidence" num="02" label="The evidence" title="A blank cell means not obtained.">
    <p>
      Thirty-one sources: BMTC audited accounts, fare charts, PRS budget compilations, FPIB studies, a
      Lokniti–CSDS evaluation, and the legislature's own replies. Every row in the seven datasets
      carries a source ID and a verification label.
    </p>
    <p>
      Three distinctions are held throughout. Budget allocations are not spending. Passenger revenue
      is not government reimbursement. A gap in the record is not a zero. The report states which one
      each number is, and lists the documents it could not obtain.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="result" num="03" label="The result" title="The farebox, and the number beside it.">
    <p>
      Farebox recovery fell from 72.2% in FY2017-18 to 34.7% in the COVID year, then recovered to
      65.5% before dropping to 60.6% in FY2024-25. The line is not a story of gradual decline; it is a
      system that never returned to its pre-pandemic base while its cost base kept growing.
    </p>

    <Fragment slot="figure">
      <Plate
        num="03"
        title="BMTC farebox recovery, FY2018–FY2025"
        caption="Traffic revenue as a share of operating expenditure, computed from the audited accounts and the FPIB series. The FY2024-25 bar is the year the report centres on."
      >
        <FareboxRecovery />
      </Plate>
    </Fragment>

    <p>
      The number beside the farebox is the one the accounts separate. BMTC's traffic revenue in
      FY2024-25 was ₹2,039.09 crore. The Shakti reimbursement is not a line item, so the report bounds
      it: ₹1,081–1,141 crore, derived from the scheme's reported ticket value and the corporation's
      traffic revenue. If that estimate is right, slightly more than half of traffic revenue is
      government money, and cash farebox recovery is 27–28%.
    </p>

    <dl class="figures">
      {figures.map(([value, label]) => (
        <div class="figures__row">
          <dt>{value}</dt>
          <dd>{label}</dd>
        </div>
      ))}
    </dl>

    <p class="mono-note">
      Also: advertising yields ₹57.54 crore a year — 2.1% of gross revenue and 8.6% of the loss.
      Non-fare revenue cannot close this gap.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="fares" num="04" label="The fare revisions" title="Both revisions were regressive with respect to distance.">
    <p>
      January 2025 raised the minimum fare by 20% and the maximum by 6.7%. November 2026 raised the
      2 km fare by 66.7% and capped the maximum increment at ₹12. The pattern is the same in both:
      the shortest journeys absorb the largest proportional increase.
    </p>
    <p>
      Set that against the reimbursement. In the scenario model, paying the Shakti claim in full is
      worth ₹1,100–1,500 crore a year; the November 2026 fare rise is worth about ₹310 crore in cash.
      The order of magnitude is the finding: reimbursement is worth four to five times the fare
      increase, and it does not fall on the passenger.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="limits" num="05" label="Known limits">
    <Limits>
      <p>
        The Shakti share is a bounded estimate, not a disclosed number. BMTC does not publish a
        Shakti receivable ledger; the range is derived from traffic revenue and the scheme's reported
        ticket value, and the report labels it as an estimate throughout.
      </p>
      <p>
        The November 2026 stage-wise fare chart had not been published at the data cut-off.
        Stage-level fares in the dataset and dashboard are interpolated from three verified anchors,
        and must not be quoted as official.
      </p>
      <p>
        FY2025-26 audited accounts were not obtained; that row is revenue only. The scenario model
        prices the reimbursement against the fare rise; it does not forecast ridership. The work is
        independent and not affiliated with the Government of Karnataka, BMTC or BMRCL.
      </p>
    </Limits>
  </Section>
</CasePage>

<style>
  .figures {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 0 clamp(24px, 4vw, 56px);
    margin-top: 28px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .figures__row {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 16px;
    padding: 12px 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .figures dt {
    order: 2;
    font-family: var(--f-display);
    font-size: clamp(17px, 1.6vw, 21px);
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .figures dd {
    order: 1;
    font-family: var(--f-mono);
    font-size: 9.5px;
    line-height: 1.4;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  @media (max-width: 620px) {
    .figures {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
  }
</style>
